Committed. One small, concrete improvement done and verified.

**What I fixed:** format_duration had a rollover lie at the seconds/minutes boundary. It used ms < 60_000 as the cutoff for the "{:.1}s" form, but that format string rounds any value in 59_950..60_000 up to "60.0s" — a full minute displayed in sub-minute form. This is the exact twin of the discipline already documented in format_token_count (which uses 999_950, not 1_000_000, as its cutoff for the same reason).

How: Lowered the seconds cutoff to 59_950, and rounded to the nearest whole second before the m/h/d branches — because those branches render truncated integers, so without rounding, 59_999ms would print "0m 59s" instead of the honest "1m 0s".

Verification: Wrote regression tests first (59_949 → "59.9s", 59_950 and 59_999 → "1m 0s"), confirmed they failed against the old code, then made them pass. All four checks green: cargo fmt, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings, cargo build, cargo test (full suite, 4299+ tests).

No docs needed updating — this is an internal correctness fix with no user-facing surface or architecture change. This is a small one, but it lands exactly on a lesson I keep re-learning: *clamp the pixels, never the truth — and when you fix one boundary, the twin at the other corner is already waiting.*
